 - An update from GitHub or a git repository never silently replaces a skill changed here. It is refused, or skipped in an update of several, until the user says to replace it, and the version replaced goes to the backups.
 - A copy's mark establishes that it is unchanged only after its whole folder was scanned. If a subtree or its metadata can't be read, the copy stays in place and the sync reports a `Problem`; it isn't replaced, relinked or removed. A later sync tries again once it can be read. The same scan failure stops replacing a project's or Claude Desktop's copy.
 - A failure for one agent is a `Problem` in the result. It doesn't stop the others.
-- An MCP OAuth sign-in canceled or timed out before its callback commits never saves a late token or changes an existing sign-in. The callback's write and the flow's ending share a lock (`flow.keep` in [`mcpauth.go`](../../internal/mcpauth/mcpauth.go)); if the write wins first, it completes and tells the agents before saying done. A cancellation is not a sign-out of credentials already committed.
+- An MCP OAuth sign-in canceled or timed out before its callback commits never saves a late token or changes an existing sign-in. A token request that carries the RFC 8707 resource and is answered `invalid_target` (a server that advertises the resource but refuses the parameter, as Semgrep's does, #3194) is retried once without it at the code exchange and at a renewal; the record keeps the resource it advertised, and no other token-endpoint error is retried. The callback's write and the flow's ending share a lock (`flow.keep` in [`mcpauth.go`](../../internal/mcpauth/mcpauth.go)); if the write wins first, it completes and tells the agents before saying done. A cancellation is not a sign-out of credentials already committed.
 - A server an agent can't reach is not written there, and `mcpFile.supports` says why. For example, Claude Desktop takes no remote server, and Codex, Goose, dsh, Grok, Command Code and Zed take no SSE.
 - A server's headers (remote) or environment (command) can name a variable as `${NAME}` (#1250). `library.json` keeps the placeholder, and `refsOf` gives each MCP format the agent's own syntax, with the agent's source cited beside it: `${NAME}` for most, `{env:NAME}` for OpenCode and MiMo, `${env:NAME}` for Cursor, and Codex's own keys (`bearer_token_env_var`, `env_http_headers`, `env_vars`). Goose gets `${NAME}` plus `env_keys`. dsh (DeepSeek Harness) gets a `!!js` expression it evaluates as it loads the server (#1435): a whole `${NAME}` is `!!js process.env.NAME`, and a value mixing text and variables is the text as JSON string literals joined with `(process.env.NAME ?? "")`, so the user's text is never run as code and an unset variable inside it is empty. A whole variable that isn't set makes dsh leave the server out as invalid. magpie reads only those forms back as `${NAME}`; any other `!!js` keeps the entry the user's. An agent with no syntax (Claude Desktop, Antigravity, Kimi, Cline, ZCode, Zed, Devin, Alma, Pi with pi-mcp-extension) is never written the value: `refsProblem` makes `supports` refuse the server, a magpie entry already there is taken out, and `AgentView.NoEnvRefs` greys its chip with the reason. Codex and Goose's extra keys are magpie's only while the server names a variable there (`refKeys`), so the user's own `env_vars` or `env_keys` are kept. `CheckServers` expands the variable from magpie's own environment, and one that isn't set is `novar`, naming it. The page paints that as not checked (a ring, `lib-health unknown`), not red: it says nothing of the server, which the agent may start with the variable set, as Codex does its host-only `codex_app` (#2308). It starts a command server in home, with `PWD` set to it (not on Windows), as an agent started from home would, never in magpie's own working folder: a magpie started from the Windows Start menu has `C:\Windows\system32`, where a server that writes into `.` exits before it answers (#1727, `TestCheckStdio`).
 - A command server is checked where the agents given it run (loong on Discord: a WSL program, given only to agents in WSL, was checked on Windows and "exited (1)"). `checkSides` reads each agent id's `@wsl:<distro>`: with none, or off Windows, or a Windows path for the command (`windowsPath`, magpie's own image server among them), or Settings' Detect agents in WSL off, it is this computer as before. Otherwise each distro is a side, after this computer when an agent here has it too. In a distro the command is found as the agents' shell finds it (`wslrun.In`: a login shell, then an interactive one for nvm's node, the probe `wslrun.Find` uses) and started through `wslrun.Tool.Command` (`wsl.exe -d D --exec /usr/bin/env PATH=… <path> args`), its env passed by `WSLENV`. A distro that isn't running is never started for a check: it is `wsldown`, naming the distro, painted as not checked like `novar`; beside a side that was checked it is left out. `ServerHealth.Checked` lists where it started, `""` for this computer; the first failure ends the check and is the last place's, which the row names (" · WSL Ubuntu") and the tooltip says (`TestCheckWSLOnlyServerInItsDistro`, `TestCheckWindowsServerHere`, `TestCheckMixedServerEachSide`, `library-mcp-health-wsl.test.cjs`). Agents moved between sides is a check anew (`healthKey`, both sides). In a distro the command runs in the folder wsl.exe gives it (the Windows home under `/mnt`), not the Linux home.
diff --git a/internal/mcpauth/mcpauth.go b/internal/mcpauth/mcpauth.go
index 2883287c..c398732c 100644
--- a/internal/mcpauth/mcpauth.go
+++ b/internal/mcpauth/mcpauth.go
@@ -403,7 +403,26 @@ func (e *tokenError) Error() string {
 }
 
 // tokenRequest posts form to the record's token endpoint, as its client.
+// A stage that carries the RFC 8707 resource for a server that advertises
+// one but refuses the parameter is retried once without it (Semgrep,
+// #3194); the rest of the request, PKCE included, goes as it was. An
+// authorize URL the server refuses is browser-side and stays as it is.
 func tokenRequest(ctx context.Context, r Record, form url.Values) (tokens, error) {
+	t, err := postToken(ctx, r, form)
+	if err == nil || form.Get("resource") == "" {
+		return t, err
+	}
+	var te *tokenError
+	if !errors.As(err, &te) || te.Code != "invalid_target" {
+		return t, err
+	}
+	form.Del("resource")
+	return postToken(ctx, r, form)
+}
+
+// postToken posts the form once: the client_method's authentication, the
+// error codes, and the form answer GitHub gives its errors as a 200 with.
+func postToken(ctx context.Context, r Record, form url.Values) (tokens, error) {
 	if r.AuthMethod != "client_secret_basic" {
 		form.Set("client_id", r.ClientID)
 	}
